  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"111\" data-end=\"253\"><strong data-start=\"111\" data-end=\"140\">Fluid protein measurement</strong> is critical in evaluating body fluids like <strong data-start=\"184\" data-end=\"239\">pleural, peritoneal (ascitic), or pericardial fluid</strong> to determine:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"255\" data-end=\"584\">\n<li data-start=\"255\" data-end=\"399\">\n<p data-start=\"257\" data-end=\"399\">Whether the fluid is a <strong data-start=\"280\" data-end=\"294\">transudate</strong> (due to systemic factors like heart failure) or an <strong data-start=\"346\" data-end=\"357\">exudate</strong> (due to local inflammation or malignancy)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"400\" data-end=\"483\">\n<p data-start=\"402\" data-end=\"483\">The <strong data-start=\"406\" data-end=\"427\">cause of effusion</strong> by assessing capillary permeability or oncotic pressure</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"484\" data-end=\"584\">\n<p data-start=\"486\" data-end=\"584\">The <strong data-start=\"490\" data-end=\"524\">severity and nature of disease</strong>, especially in infections, malignancies, or liver disorders</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"586\" data-end=\"675\">It is commonly used in conjunction with <strong data-start=\"626\" data-end=\"643\">serum protein</strong> to calculate gradients such as:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"676\" data-end=\"760\">\n<li data-start=\"676\" data-end=\"719\">\n<p data-start=\"678\" data-end=\"719\"><strong data-start=\"678\" data-end=\"719\">Serum-Ascitic Albumin Gradient (SAAG)</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"720\" data-end=\"760\">\n<p data-start=\"722\" data-end=\"760\"><strong data-start=\"722\" data-end=\"742\">Light&rsquo;s Criteria</strong> for pleural fluid</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What It Suggests</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Exudative effusions</strong></td>\n<td>High fluid protein suggests inflammation, malignancy, or infection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Malignancy</strong></td>\n<td>Cancer cells increase vascular permeability, allowing more protein into fluid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tuberculosis or bacterial infection</strong></td>\n<td>Infections often lead to exudates with high protein levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pancreatitis-related effusion</strong></td>\n<td>May present with elevated protein due to inflammatory leakage</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
